## Solution

Let $\omega = e^{i\theta}$ with $\theta \in \mathbb{R}$. We compute $|z| = |\omega+2|^3|\omega-3|^2$.

**Computing the two factors.**

$$|\omega+2|^2 = (\cos\theta+2)^2+\sin^2\theta = 5+4\cos\theta,$$

$$|\omega-3|^2 = (\cos\theta-3)^2+\sin^2\theta = 10-6\cos\theta.$$

Setting $c = \cos\theta \in [-1,1]$, we maximize

$$f(c) = (5+4c)^{3/2}(10-6c).$$

**Optimization.** Take the log and differentiate:

$$g(c) = \ln f(c) = \tfrac{3}{2}\ln(5+4c)+\ln(10-6c),$$

$$g'(c) = \frac{6}{5+4c}-\frac{6}{10-6c}.$$

Setting $g'(c)=0$:

$$\frac{1}{5+4c}=\frac{1}{10-6c}\implies 5+4c=10-6c\implies c=\frac{1}{2}.$$

This is a maximum: for $c<\tfrac12$, $5+4c<10-6c$ so $g'(c)>0$; for $c>\tfrac12$, $g'(c)<0$.

**Evaluating.** At $c=\tfrac12$:

$$5+4\cdot\tfrac12 = 7,\qquad 10-6\cdot\tfrac12 = 7,$$

$$f\!\left(\tfrac12\right)=7^{3/2}\cdot 7 = 7^{5/2}=49\sqrt{7}.$$

**Checking endpoints.** At $c=1$: $f=9^{3/2}\cdot4=108<49\sqrt7\approx129.6$. At $c=-1$: $f=1\cdot16=16$. Both are smaller, confirming the interior maximum.

The maximum length is

$$\boxed{49\sqrt{7}}.$$

